> Can't that be taken from context? (What exactly is SI?)

Not always. There have been disputes about this for a while, since 
hardware makers like to use either one, according to which one suits 
them best (e.g. a 40GB harddisk often contains 40*10^9 bytes, and not 
~43*10^9, like it should if 'computer' gigabytes were used).

And SI is 'Le Système International d'Unités', the internation system of 
units as used in most places where the metric system is used. It defines 
the units used to measure length, time, etc, as well as the prefixes for 
different powers of 10^3.

